1
00:00:00,775 --> 00:00:05,630
Three meters of timber, and
one line down its whole length.

2
00:00:06,080 --> 00:00:09,450
No straightedge that long, no pencil.

3
00:00:10,080 --> 00:00:12,790
Just a string, and a snap.

4
00:00:14,667 --> 00:00:18,377
Every kind of line on a
timber has its own tool.

5
00:00:19,027 --> 00:00:21,077
This episode, we meet them.

6
00:00:26,033 --> 00:00:30,883
The string comes from a sumitsubo,
the carpenter's ink line.

7
00:00:31,373 --> 00:00:38,303
At the front is a small pond, the ike,
packed with silk floss soaked in ink.

8
00:00:39,033 --> 00:00:41,863
Behind it, a reel holds the line.

9
00:00:42,393 --> 00:00:45,563
At the line's end is a pin, the karuko.

10
00:00:46,313 --> 00:00:50,923
Traditional pots were carved from
mulberry; zelkova is common today.

11
00:00:54,900 --> 00:00:57,770
Push the pin into one end of the mark.

12
00:00:58,340 --> 00:01:04,110
Draw the line out through the inked
floss, and pull it taut to the other end.

13
00:01:04,300 --> 00:01:08,250
Lift it straight up, and let it go.

14
00:01:09,380 --> 00:01:13,100
The line slaps down and
leaves a straight ink

15
00:01:13,140 --> 00:01:17,070
line, even across a
slightly uneven surface.

16
00:01:17,967 --> 00:01:20,557
A Western chalk line works the same way.

17
00:01:20,887 --> 00:01:22,837
The difference is what's left behind:

18
00:01:23,427 --> 00:01:24,977
chalk wipes off easily.

19
00:01:26,027 --> 00:01:30,717
Ink pots come in sizes from about
eighteen to thirty centimeters long.

20
00:01:33,433 --> 00:01:38,083
For everything shorter, there's
the sumisashi, the ink pen.

21
00:01:38,753 --> 00:01:41,403
It's a strip of bamboo, flattened.

22
00:01:42,253 --> 00:01:46,213
One end is cut on a
slant and split into fine

23
00:01:46,253 --> 00:01:51,023
slits, so it holds ink
and draws a clean line.

24
00:01:51,267 --> 00:01:54,807
The other end is rounded
and crushed into a

25
00:01:54,847 --> 00:01:58,197
little brush, for writing marks and words.

26
00:01:58,767 --> 00:02:03,177
The notes on the story stick in the
first episode were written this way.

27
00:02:03,867 --> 00:02:06,677
Now, the promise from last time:

28
00:02:07,107 --> 00:02:13,017
how to square a line with a flat
steel square that has no thick stock.

29
00:02:13,300 --> 00:02:18,900
One arm sticks out past the edge of
the board, its inner edge pressed

30
00:02:18,940 --> 00:02:23,910
against the side, held by your
fingers near the middle of that arm.

31
00:02:24,900 --> 00:02:29,450
The other arm lies across the
face, and you draw along it.

32
00:02:29,667 --> 00:02:33,697
Simply laying the square on
top of the board doesn't work:

33
00:02:34,127 --> 00:02:36,077
nothing holds it square to the edge.

34
00:02:36,567 --> 00:02:41,457
The hooked edge is your reference,
so it must be straight and true.

35
00:02:42,067 --> 00:02:45,317
And which arm hooks the edge
varies; you'll see both.

36
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:55,610
For lines parallel to an edge,
there's the kebiki, the marking gauge.

37
00:02:56,320 --> 00:03:01,400
A fence slides along a planed
edge, and a small blade at

38
00:03:01,440 --> 00:03:05,570
the end of the beam, sometimes
a pin, scores a line.

39
00:03:06,420 --> 00:03:10,040
Set the distance once, and
the gauge repeats it on every

40
00:03:10,080 --> 00:03:13,790
piece, just as the story
stick repeats lengths.

41
00:03:14,567 --> 00:03:18,407
Because it usually cuts
with a blade, it's closest

42
00:03:18,447 --> 00:03:21,897
to a Western cutting
gauge, not a pin gauge.

43
00:03:22,587 --> 00:03:25,857
A pin tends to tear the
fibers across the grain.

44
00:03:26,407 --> 00:03:28,377
A blade slices them.

45
00:03:32,433 --> 00:03:34,083
There are many kinds.

46
00:03:34,733 --> 00:03:37,503
The sujikebiki just scores lines.

47
00:03:38,433 --> 00:03:43,123
The warikebiki cuts deeper,
to split thin boards to width.

48
00:03:46,400 --> 00:03:50,030
And for the finest lines, a marking knife:

49
00:03:50,500 --> 00:03:53,650
the shiragaki, also called shirabiki.

50
00:03:54,420 --> 00:03:58,550
Most are flat on the back
and beveled on one side.

51
00:03:58,833 --> 00:04:03,943
A knife line is a fine cut,
not just a mark on the surface.

52
00:04:04,873 --> 00:04:08,443
When and how to use one
comes with the cutting tools.

53
00:04:09,300 --> 00:04:12,940
Next time, we go back
to the board plan, and

54
00:04:12,980 --> 00:04:16,210
follow the lines from
the plan onto the timber.

55
00:04:17,233 --> 00:04:19,193
Snap the long lines.

56
00:04:19,233 --> 00:04:20,803
Write with the pen.

57
00:04:21,773 --> 00:04:29,043
Square with the sashigane, gauge
the parallels, and knife the finest.

58
00:04:29,953 --> 00:04:31,883
Every line has its tool.
